Priya (AE): Hey team — Orchid Health needs a short POC. Requirements: streaming chat with function/tool calls to their appointment API, structured JSON output (strict schema), SSO (SAML) + customer-managed KMS. SLA: ~200ms tail for interactive flows, expected peak ~1k qps but bursty. Pricing question: rough hosted vs Dedicated/reserved commit for ~10M tokens/month. They also sent a security questionnaire (SOC2 + KMS questions). Can someone own tech answers and a quick SDK streaming + function_call sample we can share?

Ben (Solutions): I can take the SDK sample + runbook. Quick clarifying q: is the structured output a strict schema (validated) or lenient JSON? Also, do they expect synchronous webhook callbacks from our tool-calls or just ingestion on their side?

Priya (AE): Strict schema; they want us to validate shape server-side and return a single JSON object per response. Tool calls should be sync webhook -> Orchid's internal API.

Ben (Solutions): OK. For streaming + function_call we usually do an SSE stream where we emit partial tokens, then a final `function_call` event with arguments. Here's a minimal JS sample we can paste into the POC repo:

Ben (Solutions): ```js
// pseudo-code, Redwood JS SDK style
const stream = client.chat.stream({
  model: 'redwood-stream-1',
  messages: [{role: 'user', content: 'Book appointment for 2026-04-02 10:00'}],
  functions: [{name: 'create_appointment', schema: {/* json schema */}}],
  stream: true,
});
for await (const chunk of stream) {
  if (chunk.type === 'function_call') {
    // call Orchid API synchronously
    await fetch('https://orchid.internal/appointments', {method: 'POST', body: chunk.args});
  } else if (chunk.type === 'final') {
    // validated final JSON payload
    validateSchema(chunk.json, appointmentSchema);
  }
}
```

Ben (Solutions): I'll add a README that explains how to wire the webhook and a tiny express app that verifies signatures.

Tom (SRE): Re: KMS & SSO. For customer-managed KMS we can support two paths: Dedicated (bring-your-kms via IAM/KMS integration) or Private (VPC + customer KMS). Hosted can use envelope encryption but customers often want Private/Dedicated for full control/audit. For SAML SSO we have example SAML metadata and an IdP handshake runbook.

Tom (SRE): Link to runbook: https://redwood.internal/runbooks/presales-kms-saml :link: (I'll paste the sanitized public version into the POC repo)

Jess (Product): On latency: if they need <200ms tail for interactive tokens, recommend:
- Use a smaller optimized decoder (quantized 4-bit) and model variant tuned for low-latency
- Enable KV/prefix caching for multi-turn chat
- Keep batching low for interactive routes, but set continuous batching for background tasks
I'll provide a suggested config from Redwood Optimize for that workload.

Ellen (Sales Eng): I'll run the pricing calc, but high-level guidance: Hosted is easiest to start (no infra commit). For sustained 10M tokens/mo, Dedicated reserved capacity can be > 20% cheaper on unit costs depending on GPU class and commit length. I'll surface a concrete monthly committed tier and a one-page cost comparison by EOD.

Priya (AE): Great. Action items: Ben -> sample repo + README + signature example; Tom -> public-safe answers for KMS/SAML + excerpt for security questionnaire; Jess -> latency config + Optimize suggestions; Ellen -> hosted vs Dedicated price sheet. I'll schedule a 30m sync for tomorrow to review collateral. Owning assignee: Priya.

deploy-bot: A new doc was uploaded to the POC repo: docs/orchid-poc/readme.md

Ben (Solutions): Will add schema validation tests (unit) and a demo harness that simulates Orchid webhook latencies.

Tom (SRE): I'll also note any network egress constraints for their VPC and a checklist for IP allowlisting.

Ellen (Sales Eng): Quick ask — do we have precedent for a customer-managed HSM footprint in Dedicated? If yes I'll include it in the pricing/legal appendix.

Jess (Product): We do for Private + Dedicated with Partner HSM integration; I'll include the policy snippet.

Priya (AE): Thanks all. If anyone hits blockers, ping me and I'll escalate. Meeting invite inbound. :calendar:
